(CHAPTER I.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(DIRECTIONS ABOUT MARRIAGE; FOR CHOICE AND CONTRACT.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(AS the persons of christians in their privatest capacities are holy,) Tj T*
(as being dedicated and separated unto God, so also must their families) Tj T*
(be: HOLINESS TO THE LORD must be as it were written on their doors,) Tj T*
(and on their relations, their possessions, and affairs. To which it is) Tj T*
(requisite, 1. That there be a holy constitution of their families. 2.) Tj T*
(And a holy government of them, and discharge of the several duties of) Tj T*
(the members of the family. To the right constituting of a family,) Tj T*
(belongeth, \(1.\) The right contracting of marriage, and, \(2.\) The) Tj T*
(right choice and contract betwixt masters and their servants. For the) Tj T*
(first,) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ I. Take heed that neither lust nor rashness do thrust) Tj T*
(you into a married condition, before you see such reasons to invite) Tj T*
(you to it, as may assure you of the call and approbation of God. For,) Tj T*
(1. It is God that you must serve in your married state, and therefore) Tj T*
(it is meet that you take his counsel before you rush upon it; for he) Tj T*
(knoweth best himself what belongeth to his service. 2. And it is God) Tj T*
(that you must still depend upon, for the blessing and comforts of your) Tj T*
(relation: and therefore there is very great reason that you take his) Tj T*
(advice and consent, as the chief things requisite to the match: if the) Tj T*
(consent of parents be necessary, much more is the consent of God.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Quest._ But how shall a man know whether God call him to) Tj T*
(marriage, or consent unto it? Hath he not here left all men to their) Tj T*
(liberties, as in a thing indifferent?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Whether marriage be indifferent.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ God hath not made any universal law commanding or) Tj T*
ET
