(forbidding marriage; but in this regard hath left it indifferent to) Tj T*
(mankind: yet not allowing all to marry \(for undoubtedly to some it is) Tj T*
(unlawful\). But he hath by other general laws or rules directed men to) Tj T*
(know, in what cases it is lawful, and in what cases it is a sin. As) Tj T*
(every man is bound to choose that condition in which he may serve God) Tj T*
(with the best advantages, and which tendeth most to his spiritual) Tj T*
(welfare, and increase in holiness. Now there is nothing in marriage) Tj T*
(itself which maketh it commonly inconsistent with these benefits, and) Tj T*
(the fulfilling of these laws: and therefore it is said, that "he that) Tj T*
(marrieth doth well,"[1] that is, he doth that which of itself is not) Tj T*
(unlawful, and which to some is the most eligible state of life. But) Tj T*
(there is something in a single life which maketh it, especially to) Tj T*
(preachers and persecuted christians, to be more usually the most) Tj T*
(advantageous state of life, to these ends of christianity; and) Tj T*
(therefore it is said, that "he that marrieth not, doth better." And) Tj T*
(yet to individual persons, it is hard to imagine how it can choose but) Tj T*
(be either a duty or a sin; at least except in some unusual cases. For) Tj T*
(it is a thing of so great moment as to the ordering of our hearts and) Tj T*
(lives, that it is hard to imagine that it should ever be indifferent) Tj T*
(as a means to our main end, but must either be a very great help or) Tj T*
(hinderance. But yet if there be any persons whose case may be so) Tj T*
(equally poised with accidents on both sides, that to the most) Tj T*
(judicious man it is not discernible, whether a single or married state) Tj T*
(of life is like to conduce more to their personal holiness or public) Tj T*
(usefulness, or the good of others, to such persons marriage in the) Tj T*
(individual circumstantiated act is a thing indifferent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Who are called to marry.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(By these conditions following you may know, what persons have a call) Tj T*
(from God to marry, and who have not his call or approbation. 1. If) Tj T*
(there be the peremptory will or command of parents to children that) Tj T*
(are under their power and government, and no greater matter on the) Tj T*
(contrary to hinder it, the command of parents signifieth the command) Tj T*
(of God: but if parents do but persuade and not command, though their) Tj T*
(desires must not be causelessly refused, yet a smaller impediment may) Tj T*
(preponderate than in case of a peremptory command. 2. They are called) Tj T*
(to marry who have not the gift of continence, and cannot by the use) Tj T*
(of lawful means attain it, and have no impediment which maketh it) Tj T*
(unlawful to them to marry. "But if they cannot contain, let them) Tj T*
(marry; for it is better to marry than to burn," 1 Cor. vii. 9. But) Tj T*
(here the divers degrees of the urgent and the hindering causes must be) Tj T*
(compared, and the weightiest must prevail. For some that have very) Tj T*
(strong lusts may yet have stronger impediments: and though they cannot) Tj T*
(keep that chastity in their thoughts as they desire, yet in such a) Tj T*
(case they must abstain. And there is no man but may keep his body in) Tj T*
(chastity if he will do his part: yea, and thoughts themselves, may be) Tj T*
(commonly, and for the most part, kept pure, and wanton imaginations) Tj T*
(quickly checked, if men be godly, and will do what they can. But on) Tj T*
(the other side, there are some that have a more tameable measure of) Tj T*
(concupiscence, and yet have no considerable hinderance, whose duty it) Tj T*
(may be to marry, as the most certain and successful means against that) Tj T*
ET
