(small degree, as long as there is nothing to forbid it. 3. Another) Tj T*
(cause that warranteth marriage is, when upon a wise casting up of all) Tj T*
(accounts, it is apparently most probable that in a married state, one) Tj T*
(may be most serviceable to God and the public good: that there will be) Tj T*
(in it greater helps and fewer hinderances to the great ends of our) Tj T*
(lives; the glorifying of God, and the saving of ourselves and others.) Tj T*
(And whereas it must be expected that every condition should be more) Tj T*
(helpful to us in one respect, and hinder us more in another respect;) Tj T*
(and that in one we have most helps for a contemplative life, and in) Tj T*
(another we are better furnished for an active, serviceable life, the) Tj T*
(great skill therefore in the discerning of our duties, lieth in the) Tj T*
(prudent pondering and comparing of the commodities and discommodities,) Tj T*
(without the seduction of fantasy, lust, or passion, and in a true) Tj T*
(discerning which side it is that hath the greatest weight.[2]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Observations.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Here it must be carefully observed, 1. That the two first reasons for) Tj T*
(marriage, \(concupiscence and the will of parents,\) or any such like,) Tj T*
(have their strength but in subordination to the third \(the final) Tj T*
(cause, or interest of God and our salvation\). And that this last) Tj T*
(reason \(from the end\) is of itself sufficient without any of the) Tj T*
(other, but none of the other are sufficient without this. If it be) Tj T*
(clear that in a married state you have better advantages for the) Tj T*
(service of God, and doing good to others, and saving your own souls,) Tj T*
(than you can have in a single state of life, then it is undoubtedly) Tj T*
(your duty to marry; for our obligation to seek our ultimate end is the) Tj T*
(most constant, indispensable obligation. Though parents command it) Tj T*
(not, though you have no corporal necessity, yet it is a duty if it) Tj T*
(certainly make most for your ultimate end. 2. But yet observe also,) Tj T*
(that no pretence of your ultimate end itself will warrant you to) Tj T*
(marry, when any other accident hath first made it a thing unlawful,) Tj T*
(while that accident continueth. For we must not do evil that good may) Tj T*
(come by it. Our salvation is not furthered by sin; and though we saw a) Tj T*
(probability that we might do more good to others, if we did but commit) Tj T*
(such a sin to accomplish it, yet it is not to be done. For our lives) Tj T*
(and mercies being all in the hand of God, and the successes and) Tj T*
(acceptance of all our endeavours depending wholly upon him, it can) Tj T*
(never be a rational way to attain them, by wilful offending him by our) Tj T*
(sin! It is a likely means to public good for able and good men to be) Tj T*
(magistrates and ministers; and yet he that would lie, or be perjured,) Tj T*
(or commit any known sin that he may be a magistrate, or that he may) Tj T*
(preach the gospel, might better expect a curse on himself and his) Tj T*
(endeavours, than God's acceptance, or his blessing and success; so he) Tj T*
(that would sin to change his state for the better, would find that he) Tj T*
(changed it for the worse: or if it do good to others, he may expect no) Tj T*
(good but ruin to himself, if repentance prevent it not. 3. Observe) Tj T*
(also, that if the question be only which state of life it is \(married) Tj T*
(or single\) which best conduceth to this ultimate end, then any one of) Tj T*
(the subordinate reasons will prove that we have a call, if there be) Tj T*
(not greater reasons on the contrary side. As in case you have no) Tj T*
(corporal necessity, the will of parents alone may oblige you, if there) Tj T*
ET
