(be no greater thing against it: or if parents oblige you not, yet) Tj T*
(corporal necessity alone may do it: or if neither of these invite you,) Tj T*
(yet a clear probability of the attaining of such an estate or) Tj T*
(opportunity, as may make you more fit to relieve many others, or be) Tj T*
(serviceable to the church, or the blessing of children who may be) Tj T*
(devoted to God, may warrant your marriage, if no greater reasons lie) Tj T*
(against it; for when the scales are equal, any one of these may turn) Tj T*
(them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Sidenote: Who may not marry.]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(By this also you may perceive who they be that have no call to marry,) Tj T*
(and to whom it is a sin. As, 1. No man hath a call to marry, who) Tj T*
(laying all the commodities and discommodities together, may clearly) Tj T*
(discern that a married state is like to be a greater hinderance of his) Tj T*
(salvation, or to his serving or honouring God in the world, and so to) Tj T*
(disadvantage him as to his ultimate end.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Quest._ But what if parents do command it? or will set against) Tj T*
(me if I disobey?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ Parents have no authority to command you any thing) Tj T*
(against God or your salvation, or your ultimate end. Therefore here) Tj T*
(you owe them no formal obedience: but yet the will of parents, with) Tj T*
(all the consequents, must be put into the scales with all other) Tj T*
(considerations, and if they make the discommodities of a single life) Tj T*
(to become the greater, as to your end, then they may bring you under a) Tj T*
(duty or obligation to marry; not _necessitate præcepti_, as) Tj T*
(obedience to their command; but _necessitate medii_, as a means) Tj T*
(to your ultimate end, and in obedience to that general command of God,) Tj T*
(which requireth you to "seek first" your ultimate end, even "the) Tj T*
(kingdom of God, and his righteousness," Matt. vi. 33.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Quest._ But what if I have a corporal necessity, and yet I can) Tj T*
(foresee that marriage will greatly disadvantage me as to the service) Tj T*
(of God and my salvation?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ 1. You must understand that no corporal necessity is) Tj T*
(absolute: for there is no man so lustful but may possibly bridle his) Tj T*
(lust by other lawful means; by diet, labour, sober company, diverting) Tj T*
(business, solitude, watching the thoughts and senses, or at least by) Tj T*
(the physician's help; so that the necessity is but _secundum) Tj T*
(quid_, or an urgency rather than a simple necessity. And then, 2.) Tj T*
(This measure of necessity must be itself laid in the balance with the) Tj T*
(other accidents; and if this necessity will turn the scales by making) Tj T*
(a single life more disadvantageous to your ultimate end, your lust) Tj T*
(being a greater impediment to you than all the inconveniencies of) Tj T*
(marriage will be, then the case is resolved, "it is better to marry) Tj T*
(than to burn." But if the hinderances in a married state are like to) Tj T*
(be greater, than the hinderances of your concupiscence, then you must) Tj T*
(set yourself to the curbing and curing of that concupiscence; and in) Tj T*
(the use of God's means expect his blessing.) Tj T*
ET
