(1. Marriage ordinarily plungeth men into excess of worldly cares; it) Tj T*
(multiplieth their business, and usually their wants. There are many) Tj T*
(things to mind and do; there are many to provide for. And many persons) Tj T*
(you will have to do with, who have all of them a selfish disposition) Tj T*
(and interest, and will judge of you but according as you fit their) Tj T*
(ends. And among many persons and businesses, some things will) Tj T*
(frequently fall cross: you must look for many rubs and disappointments.) Tj T*
(And your natures are not so strong, content, and patient, as to bear) Tj T*
(all these without molestation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. Your wants in a married state are hardlier supplied, than in a) Tj T*
(single life. You will want so many things which before you never) Tj T*
(wanted, and have so many to provide for and content, that all will) Tj T*
(seem little enough, if you had never so much. Then you will be often) Tj T*
(at your wit's end, taking thought for the future, what you shall eat,) Tj T*
(and what you shall drink, and wherewith shall you and yours be clothed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. Your wants in a married state are far hardlier borne than in a) Tj T*
(single state. It is far easier to bear personal wants ourselves, than) Tj T*
(to see the wants of wife and children: affection will make their) Tj T*
(sufferings pinch you. And ingenuity will make it a trouble to your) Tj T*
(mind, to need the help of servants, and to want that which is fit for) Tj T*
(servants to expect. But especially the discontent and impatience of) Tj T*
(your family will more discontent you than all their wants. You cannot) Tj T*
(help your wife, and children, and servants to contented minds. Oh what) Tj T*
(a heart-cutting trial is it to hear them repining, murmuring, and) Tj T*
(complaining! to hear them call for that which you have not for them,) Tj T*
(and grieve at their condition, and exclaim of you, or of the) Tj T*
(providence of God, because they have it not! And think not that riches) Tj T*
(will free you from these discontents; for as the rich are but few, so) Tj T*
(they that have much have much to do with it. A great foot must have a) Tj T*
(great shoe. When poor men want some small supplies, rich men may want) Tj T*
(great sums, or larger provisions, which the poor can easily be) Tj T*
(without. And their condition lifting them up to greater pride, doth) Tj T*
(torment them with greater discontents. How few in all the world that) Tj T*
(have families, are content with their estates!) Tj T*
() Tj T*
