(PART I.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Directions for the right Choice of Servants._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(SERVANTS being integral parts of the family, who contribute much to) Tj T*
(the holiness or unholiness of it, and to the happiness or misery of) Tj T*
(it, it much concerneth masters to be careful in their choice. And the) Tj T*
(harder it is to find such as are indeed desirable, the more careful) Tj T*
(and diligent in it should you be.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ I. To bid you choose such as are fittest for your) Tj T*
(service, is a direction which nature and interest will give you,) Tj T*
(without any persuasions of mine. And indeed it is not mere honesty or) Tj T*
(piety that will make a good servant, nor do your work. Three things) Tj T*
(are necessary to make a servant fit for you: 1. Strength. 2. Skill. 3.) Tj T*
(Willingness. And no two of these will serve without the third.) Tj T*
(Strength and skill without willingness, will do nothing: skill and) Tj T*
(willingness without strength, can do nothing: strength and willingness) Tj T*
(without skill, will do as bad or worse than nothing. No less than all) Tj T*
(will make you a good servant. Therefore choose one, 1. That is) Tj T*
(healthful. 2. That hath been used to such work as you must employ him) Tj T*
(in: and, 3. One that is not of a flesh-pleasing, or lazy, sluggish) Tj T*
(disposition. For to exact labour from one that is sickly will seem) Tj T*
(cruelty; and to expect labour from one that is unskilful and) Tj T*
(unexercised will seem folly; and heavy, fleshly, slothful persons,) Tj T*
(will do all with so much unwillingness, and pain, and weariness, that) Tj T*
(they will think all too much, and their service will be a continual) Tj T*
(toil and displeasure to them, and they will think you wrong them, or) Tj T*
(deal hardly with them, if you will not allow them in their fleshliness) Tj T*
(and idleness. Yea, though they should have grace, a phlegmatic,) Tj T*
(sluggish, heavy body, will never be fit for diligent service, any more) Tj T*
(than a tired horse for travel.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ II. If it be possible, choose such as have the fear of) Tj T*
(God, or at least such as are tractable and willing to be taught, and) Tj T*
(not such as are ungodly, sensual, and profane. For, 1. "God hateth all) Tj T*
(the workers of iniquity," Psal. v. 5. And it tendeth not to the) Tj T*
(blessing or safety of your family, to have in it such as are enemies) Tj T*
(to God, and hated by him. You cannot expect an equal blessing on their) Tj T*
(labours, as you may on the service of those that fear him. The wicked) Tj T*
(may bring a curse on the families where they are \(if you wilfully) Tj T*
(entertain them\); when a Joseph may be a blessing even to the house of) Tj T*
(an unbeliever. A wicked man will be renewing those crimes, which will) Tj T*
(be the shame of your family, and a grief to your hearts, if you have) Tj T*
(any love to God yourselves; when a godly servant will pray for a) Tj T*
(blessing from God upon his labours, and is himself under a promise,) Tj T*
(that "whatever he doth shall prosper," Psal. i. 3. 2. Ungodly servants) Tj T*
(for the most part will be mere eye-servants; they will do little more) Tj T*
(than they find necessary to escape reproof and blame: some few of) Tj T*
(them, indeed, out of love to their masters, or out of a desire of) Tj T*
(praise, or to make their places the better to themselves, will be) Tj T*
(diligent and trusty: but ordinarily they are deceitful, and study more) Tj T*
