(CHAPTER VIII.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE SPECIAL DUTIES OF HUSBANDS TO THEIR WIVES.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(HE that will expect duty or comfort from his wife, must be faithful in) Tj T*
(doing the duty of a husband. The failing of yourselves in your own) Tj T*
(duty, may cause the failing of another to you, or at least will some) Tj T*
(other way as much afflict you, and will be bitterer to you in the end,) Tj T*
(than if a hundred failed of their duty to you. A good husband will) Tj T*
(either make a good wife, or easily and profitably endure a bad one. I) Tj T*
(shall therefore give you directions for your own part of duty, as that) Tj T*
(which your happiness is most concerned in.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ I. The husband must undertake the principal part of the) Tj T*
(government of the whole family, even of the wife herself. And) Tj T*
(therefore, 1. He must labour to be fit and able for that government) Tj T*
(which he undertaketh. This ability consisteth, 1. In holiness and) Tj T*
(spiritual wisdom, that he may be acquainted with the end to which he) Tj T*
(is to conduct them, and the rule by which he is to guide them, and) Tj T*
(the principal works which they are to do. An ungodly, irreligious man) Tj T*
(is both a stranger and an enemy to the chiefest part of family) Tj T*
(government. 2. His ability consisteth in a due acquaintance with the) Tj T*
(works of his calling, and the labours in which his servants are to be) Tj T*
(employed. For he that is utterly unacquainted with their business,) Tj T*
(will be very unfit to govern them in it: unless he commit that part of) Tj T*
(their government to his wife, or a steward that is acquainted with it.) Tj T*
(3. And he must be acquainted both with the common temper and) Tj T*
(infirmities of mankind, that he may know how much is to be borne with,) Tj T*
(and also with the particular temper, and faults, and virtues of those) Tj T*
(whom he is to govern. 4. And he must have prudence, to direct himself) Tj T*
(in all his carriage to them; and justice, to deal with every one as) Tj T*
(they deserve: and love, to do them all the good he can, for soul and) Tj T*
(body. II. And being thus able, he must make it his daily work, and) Tj T*
(especially be sure that he govern himself well, that his example may) Tj T*
(be part of his government of others.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ II. The husband must so unite authority and love, that) Tj T*
(neither of them may be omitted or concealed, but both be exercised and) Tj T*
(maintained. Love must not be exercised so imprudently as to destroy) Tj T*
(the exercise of authority; and authority must not be exercised over a) Tj T*
(wife so magisterially and imperiously, as to destroy the exercise of) Tj T*
(love. As your love must be a governing love, so your commands must all) Tj T*
(be loving commands. Lose not your authority; for that will but disable) Tj T*
