(together, and you shall miss of comfort, if you cast off duty.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ I. Be specially loving to your husbands: your natures) Tj T*
(give you the advantage in this; and love feedeth love. This is your) Tj T*
(special requital for all the troubles that your infirmities put them) Tj T*
(to.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ II. Live in a voluntary subjection and obedience to) Tj T*
(them. If their softness or yieldingness cause them to relinquish their) Tj T*
(authority; and for peace they are fain to let you have your wills; yet) Tj T*
(remember that it is God that hath appointed them to be your heads and) Tj T*
(governors. If they are so silly as to be unable, you should not have) Tj T*
(chosen such to rule you as are unfit; but having chosen them, you must) Tj T*
(assist them with your better understanding, in a submissive, and not a) Tj T*
(ruling, masterly way. A servant that hath a foolish master, may help) Tj T*
(him without becoming master. And do not deceive yourselves by giving) Tj T*
(the bare titles of government to your husbands, when you must needs in) Tj T*
(all things have your own wills; for this is but mockery, and not) Tj T*
(obedience. To be subject and obedient, is to take the understanding) Tj T*
(and will of another to govern you, before \(though not without\) your) Tj T*
(own; and to make your understandings and wills to follow the conduct) Tj T*
(of his that governeth you. Self-willedness is contrary to subjection) Tj T*
(and obedience.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ III. Learn of your husbands as your appointed teachers,) Tj T*
(and be not self-conceited and wise in your own eyes, but ask of them) Tj T*
(such instructions as your case requireth. 1 Cor. xiv. 34, 35, "Let) Tj T*
(your women keep silence in the churches: for it is not permitted to) Tj T*
(them to speak; but they are commanded to be under obedience, as also) Tj T*
(saith the law: and if they will learn any thing, let them ask their) Tj T*
(husbands at home." \(Unless when the husband is so ignorant as to be) Tj T*
(utterly unable: which is his sin and shame. For it is vain to ask that) Tj T*
(of them which they know not.\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ IV. Set yourselves seriously to amend all those faults) Tj T*
(which they reprove in you. Do not take it ill to be reproved: swell) Tj T*
(not against it, as if they did you harm or wrong: it is a very ill) Tj T*
(sign to "hate reproof," Prov. xii. 1; x. 17; xv. 10, 31, 32; xvii. 10.) Tj T*
(And what doth their government of you signify, if you will not amend) Tj T*
