(consent; this is thievery, disobedience, and injustice.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Quest._ I. But as the case standeth with us in England, hath the) Tj T*
(wife a joint-propriety, or not?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ Three ways \(at least\) she may have a propriety. 1. By a) Tj T*
(reserve of what was her own before; which \(however some question it\)) Tj T*
(may in some cases be done in their agreement at marriage. 2. By the) Tj T*
(law of the land. 3. By the husband's consent or donation. What the law) Tj T*
(of the land saith in case, I leave to the lawyers; but it seemeth to) Tj T*
(me, that his words at marriage, "With all my worldly goods I thee) Tj T*
(endow," do signify his consent to make her a joint-proprietor: and his) Tj T*
(consent is sufficient to the collation of a title to that which was) Tj T*
(his own. Unless any can prove, that law or custom doth otherwise) Tj T*
(expound the words, \(as an empty formality,\) and that at the contract,) Tj T*
(this was or should be known to her to be the sense. And the laws) Tj T*
(allowing the wife the third part upon death or separation, doth) Tj T*
(intimate a joint-propriety before.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Quest._ II. If the husband live upon unlawful gain, as cheating,) Tj T*
(stealing, robbing by the high-way, &c. is not the wife guilty as a) Tj T*
(joint-proprietor, in retaining such ill-gotten goods, if she know it?) Tj T*
(And is she bound to accuse her husband, or to restore such goods?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ Her duty is first to admonish her husband of his sin and) Tj T*
(danger, and endeavour his repentance, in the mean time disclaiming all) Tj T*
