(husband; unless when any superior powers do forbid it, and that in) Tj T*
(cases where their authority is greater. \(3.\) She may modestly give her) Tj T*
(reasons of dissent. \(4.\) She must not turn it to an unpeaceable) Tj T*
(quarrel, or matter of disaffection, or pretend any differences against) Tj T*
(her conjugal duties. \(5.\) In dark and difficult cases she should not) Tj T*
(be peremptory, and self-conceited, nor importunate; but if she have) Tj T*
(faith \(that is, some more knowledge than he\) have it to herself, in) Tj T*
(quietness and silence; and seek further information lest she err. \(6.\)) Tj T*
(She must speak no untruth, nor commit any known sin, in obedience to) Tj T*
(her husband's judgment. \(7.\) When she strongly suspecteth it to be) Tj T*
(sin, she must not do it merely in obedience to him, but seek for) Tj T*
(better satisfaction. For she is sure that he hath no power to force) Tj T*
(her to sin; and therefore hath no more assurance of his power in that) Tj T*
(point than she hath of the lawfulness of the thing. \(8.\) But if she) Tj T*
(prove to be in the error, she will sin on either side, till she) Tj T*
(recover. \(9.\) If a husband be in dangerous error, she must wisely, but) Tj T*
(unweariedly, seek his reformation, by herself or others.) Tj T*
