(CHAPTER XI.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE SPECIAL DUTIES OF CHILDREN TOWARDS THEIR PARENTS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THOUGH precepts to children are not of so much force as to them of) Tj T*
(riper age, because of their natural incapacity, and their childish) Tj T*
(passions and pleasures which bear down their weak degree of reason;) Tj T*
(yet somewhat is to be said to them, because that measure of reason) Tj T*
(which they have is to be exercised, and by exercise to be improved:) Tj T*
(and because even those of riper years, while they have parents, must) Tj T*
(know and do their duty to them; and because God useth to bless even) Tj T*
(children as they perform their duties.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ I. Be sure that you dearly love your parents; delight) Tj T*
(to be in their company; be not like those unnatural children, that) Tj T*
(love the company of their idle play-fellows better than their parents,) Tj T*
(and had rather be abroad about their sports, than in their parents') Tj T*
(sight. Remember that you have your being from them, and come out of) Tj T*
(their loins: remember what sorrow you have cost them, and what care) Tj T*
(they are at for your education and provision; and remember how) Tj T*
(tenderly they have loved you, and what grief it will be to their) Tj T*
(hearts if you miscarry, and how much your happiness will make them) Tj T*
(glad: remember what love you owe them both by nature and in justice,) Tj T*
(for all their love to you, and all that they have done for you: they) Tj T*
(take your happiness or misery to be one of the greatest parts of the) Tj T*
(happiness or misery of their own lives. Deprive them not then of their) Tj T*
(happiness, by depriving yourselves of your own; make not their lives) Tj T*
(miserable, by undoing yourselves. Though they chide you, and restrain) Tj T*
(you, and correct you, do not therefore abate your love to them. For) Tj T*
(this is their duty, which God requireth of them, and they do it for) Tj T*
(your good. It is a sign of a wicked child, that loveth his parents the) Tj T*
(less because they correct him, and will not let him have his own will.) Tj T*
(Yea, though your parents have many faults themselves, yet you must) Tj T*
(love them as your parents still.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ II. Honour your parents both in your thoughts, and) Tj T*
(speeches, and behaviour. Think not dishonourably or contemptuously of) Tj T*
(them in your hearts. Speak not dishonourably, rudely, unreverently, or) Tj T*
(saucily, either to them or of them. Behave not yourselves rudely and) Tj T*
(unreverently before them. Yea, though your parents be never so poor in) Tj T*
(the world, or weak of understanding, yea, though they were ungodly,) Tj T*
(you must honour them notwithstanding all this; though you cannot) Tj T*
(honour them as rich, or wise, or godly, you must honour them as your) Tj T*
(parents. Remember that the fifth commandment hath a special promise of) Tj T*
(temporal blessing; "Honour thy father and mother that thy days may be) Tj T*
(long in the land," &c. And consequently the dishonourers of parents) Tj T*
(have a special curse even in this life: and the justice of God is) Tj T*
(ordinarily seen in the execution of it; the despisers and dishonourers) Tj T*
(of their parents seldom prosper in the world. There are five sorts of) Tj T*
