(your hearts to him reverently and thankfully for the rest of the night) Tj T*
(past, and briefly cast yourselves upon him for the following day; and) Tj T*
(use yourselves so constantly to this, that your consciences may check) Tj T*
(you, when common thoughts shall first intrude. And if you have a) Tj T*
(bed-fellow to speak to, let your first speech be agreeable to your) Tj T*
(thoughts. It will be a great help against the temptations that may) Tj T*
(else surprise you, and a holy engagement of your hearts to God, for) Tj T*
(all the day.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ III. Resolve, that pride and the fashions of the times) Tj T*
(shall never tempt you into such a garb of attire, as will make you) Tj T*
(long in dressing you in the morning; but wear such clothing as is soon) Tj T*
(put on. It is dear-bought bravery \(or decency as they will needs call) Tj T*
(it\) which must cost every day an hour's or a quarter of an hour's time) Tj T*
(extraordinary: I had rather go as the wild Indians, than have those) Tj T*
