(CHAPTER XIX.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(DIRECTIONS FOR PROFITABLE HEARING THE WORD PREACHED.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(OMITTING those directions which concern the external modes of worship,) Tj T*
(\(for the reasons mentioned part. iii. and known to all that know me) Tj T*
(and the time and place I live in,\) I shall give you such directions) Tj T*
(about the personal, internal management of your duty, as I think most) Tj T*
(necessary to your edification. And seeing that your duty and benefit) Tj T*
(lieth in these four general points: 1. That you hear with) Tj T*
(understanding. 2. That you remember what you hear. 3. That you be duly) Tj T*
(affected with it. 4. And that you sincerely practise it: I shall more) Tj T*
(particularly direct you in order to all these ends and duties.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Tit. 1. Directions for the Understanding the Word which you hear._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ I. Read and meditate on the holy Scriptures much in) Tj T*
(private, and then you will be the better able to understand what is) Tj T*
(preached on it in public, and to try the doctrine, whether it be of) Tj T*
(God. Whereas if you are unacquainted with the Scriptures, all that is) Tj T*
(treated of or alleged from them, will be so strange to you, that you) Tj T*
(will be but little edified by it, Psal. i. 2; cxix.; Deut. vi. 11, 12.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ II. Live under the clearest, distinct, convincing) Tj T*
(teaching that possibly you can procure. There is an unspeakable) Tj T*
(difference as to the edification of the hearers, between a judicious,) Tj T*
(clear, distinct, and skilful preacher, and one that is ignorant,) Tj T*
(confused, general, dry, and only scrapeth together a cento or) Tj T*
(mingle-mangle of some undigested sayings to fill up the hour with. If) Tj T*
(in philosophy, physics, grammar, law, and every art and science, there) Tj T*
(be so great a difference between one teacher and another, it must) Tj T*
(needs be so in divinity also. Ignorant teachers, that understand not) Tj T*
(what they say themselves, are unlike to make you men of understanding;) Tj T*
(as erroneous teachers are unlike to make you orthodox and sound.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ III. Come not to hear with a careless heart, as if you) Tj T*
(were to hear a matter that little concerned you, but come with a sense) Tj T*
(of the unspeakable weight, necessity, and consequence of the holy word) Tj T*
(which you are to hear: and when you understand how much you are) Tj T*
(concerned in it, and truly love it, as the word of life, it will) Tj T*
(greatly help your understanding of every particular truth. That which) Tj T*
(a man loveth not, and perceiveth no necessity of, he will hear with so) Tj T*
(little regard and heed, that it will make no considerable impression) Tj T*
(on his mind. But a good understanding of the excellency and necessity,) Tj T*
(exciting love and serious attention, would make the particulars easy) Tj T*
(to be understood; when else you will be like a stopped or) Tj T*
(narrow-mouthed bottle, that keepeth out that which you desire to put) Tj T*
(in. I know that understanding must go before affections; but yet the) Tj T*
