(_Tit. 2. Directions for Remembering what you Hear._) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That want of memory, which cometh from age and decay of nature, is not) Tj T*
(to be cured; nor should any servant of Christ be over-much troubled at) Tj T*
(it; seeing Christ will no more cast off his servants for that, than he) Tj T*
(will for age or any sickness: but for that want of memory which is) Tj T*
(curable, and is a fault, I shall give you these Directions following.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ I. It greatly helpeth memory to have a full understanding of) Tj T*
(the matter spoken which you would remember. And ignorance is one of) Tj T*
(the greatest hinderances to memory. Common experience telleth you) Tj T*
(this, how easily you can remember any discourse which you thoroughly) Tj T*
(understand \(for your very knowledge by invention will revive your) Tj T*
(memory\); and how hard it is to remember any words which are) Tj T*
(insignificant, or which we understand not. Therefore labour most for a) Tj T*
(clear understanding according to the last directions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ II. A deep, awakened affection is a very powerful help) Tj T*
(to memory. We easily remember any thing which our estates or lives lie) Tj T*
(on, when trifles are neglected and soon forgotten. Therefore labour to) Tj T*
(get all to your hearts, according to the next following directions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ III. Method is a very great help to memory. Therefore) Tj T*
(be acquainted with the preacher's method; and then you are put into a) Tj T*
(path or tract, which you cannot easily go out of. And therefore it is,) Tj T*
(that ministers must not only be methodical, and avoid prolix,) Tj T*
(confused, and involved discourses, and that malicious pride of hiding) Tj T*
(their method, but must be as oft in the use of the same method, as the) Tj T*
(subject will bear, and choose that method which is most easy to the) Tj T*
