(BE MOST LIKELY TO HAVE SUCCESS.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(I HERE suppose them utterly untaught that you have to do with; and) Tj T*
(therefore shall direct you what to do, from the very first beginning) Tj T*
(of your teaching, and their learning. And I beseech you study this) Tj T*
(chapter more than many of the rest; for it is an unspeakable loss that) Tj T*
(befalls the church, and the souls of men, for want of skill, and will,) Tj T*
(and diligence, in parents and masters in this matter.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ I. Cause your younger children to learn the words,) Tj T*
(though they be not yet capable of understanding the matter. And do not) Tj T*
(think as some do, that this is but to make them hypocrites, and to) Tj T*
(teach them to take God's name in vain: for it is neither vanity nor) Tj T*
(hypocrisy to help them first to understand the words and signs, in) Tj T*
(order to their early understanding of the matter and signification.) Tj T*
(Otherwise no man might teach them any language, nor teach them to read) Tj T*
(any words that be good, because they must first understand the words) Tj T*
(before the meaning. If a child learn to read in a Bible, it is not) Tj T*
(taking God's name or word in vain, though he understand it not; for it) Tj T*
(is in order to his learning to understand it; and it is not vain which) Tj T*
(is to so good a use: if you leave them untaught till they come to be) Tj T*
(twenty years of age, they must then learn the words before they can) Tj T*
(understand the matter. Do not therefore leave them the children of) Tj T*
(darkness, for fear of making them hypocrites. It will be an excellent) Tj T*
(way to redeem their time, to teach them first that which they are) Tj T*
(capable of learning: a child of five or six years old can learn the) Tj T*
ET
