(words of a catechism or Scripture, before they are capable of) Tj T*
(understanding them. And then when they come to years of understanding,) Tj T*
(that part of their work is done, and they have nothing to do but to) Tj T*
(study the meaning and use of those words which they have learned) Tj T*
(already. Whereas if you leave them utterly untaught till then, they) Tj T*
(must then be wasting a long time to learn the same words which they) Tj T*
(might have learned before; and the loss of so much time is no small) Tj T*
(loss or sin.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ II. The most natural way of teaching children the) Tj T*
(meaning of God's word, and the matters of their salvation, is by) Tj T*
(familiar talk with them suited to their capacities: begin this betimes) Tj T*
(with them while they are on their mother's laps, and use it) Tj T*
(frequently. For they are quickly capable of some understanding about) Tj T*
(greater matters as well as about less; and knowledge must come in by) Tj T*
(slow degrees: stay not till their minds are prepossessed with vanity) Tj T*
(and toys, Prov. xxii. 6.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ III. By all means let your children learn to read,) Tj T*
(though you be never so poor, whatever shift you make. And if you have) Tj T*
(servants that cannot read, let them learn yet, \(at spare hours,\) if) Tj T*
(they be of any capacity and willingness. For it is a very great mercy) Tj T*
(to be able to read the holy Scripture, and any good books themselves,) Tj T*
(and a very great misery to know nothing but what they hear from) Tj T*
(others. They may read almost at any time, when they cannot hear.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ IV. Let your children when they are little ones read much) Tj T*
(the history of the Scriptures. For though this, of itself, is not) Tj T*
(sufficient to breed in them any saving knowledge, yet it enticeth them) Tj T*
(to delight in reading the Bible, and then they will be often at it) Tj T*
(when they love it; so that all these benefits will follow. 1. It will) Tj T*
(make them love the book \(though it be but with a common love\). 2. It) Tj T*
(will make them spend their time in it, when else they would rather be) Tj T*
(at play. 3. It will acquaint them with Scripture history, which will) Tj T*
(afterwards be very useful to them. 4. It will lead them up by degrees) Tj T*
(to the knowledge of the doctrine, which is all along interwoven with) Tj T*
(the history.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ V. Take heed that you turn not all your family) Tj T*
(instructions into a customary, formal course, by bare readings and) Tj T*
(repeating sermons from day to day, without familiar personal) Tj T*
(application. For it is ordinarily seen that they will grow as sleepy,) Tj T*
(and senseless, and customary, under such a dull and distant course of) Tj T*
(duty, \(though the matter be good,\) almost as if you had said nothing) Tj T*
(to them. Your business therefore must be to get within them, and) Tj T*
(awaken their consciences to know that the matter doth most nearly) Tj T*
(concern them, and to force them to make application of it to) Tj T*
(themselves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ VI. Let none affect a formal, preaching way to their) Tj T*
(families, except they be preachers themselves, or men that are able) Tj T*
(for the ministry: but rather spend the time in reading to them the) Tj T*
