(powerfullest books, and speaking to them more familiarly about the) Tj T*
(state and matters of their souls. Not that I think it unlawful for a) Tj T*
(man to preach to his family, in the same method that a minister doth) Tj T*
(to his people; for no doubt he may teach them in the profitablest) Tj T*
(manner he can; and that which is the best method for a set speech in) Tj T*
(the pulpit, is usually the best method in a family. But my reasons) Tj T*
(against this preaching way ordinarily, are these:--1. Because it is) Tj T*
(very few masters of families that are able for it \(even among them) Tj T*
(that think they are\); and then they ignorantly abuse the Scripture, so) Tj T*
(as tends much to God's dishonour. 2. Because there is scarce any of) Tj T*
(them all, but may read at the same time, such lively, profitable books) Tj T*
(to their families, as handle those things which they have most need to) Tj T*
(hear of, in a far more edifying manner than they themselves are able) Tj T*
(\(except they be so poor that they can get no such books\). 3. Because) Tj T*
(the familiar way is most edifying; and to talk seriously with children) Tj T*
(and servants about the great concernments of their souls, doth) Tj T*
(commonly more move them than sermons or set speeches. Yet because) Tj T*
(there is a season for both, you may sometimes read some powerful book) Tj T*
(to them, and sometimes talk familiarly to them. 4. Because it often) Tj T*
(comes from pride, when men put their speech into a preaching method to) Tj T*
(show their parts, and as often nourisheth pride.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ VII. Let the manner of your teaching them be very often) Tj T*
(interlocutory, or by way of questions. Though when you have so many or) Tj T*
(such persons present, as that such familiarity is not seasonable, then) Tj T*
(reading, repeating, or set speeches may do best; but at other times,) Tj T*
(when the number or quality of the company hindereth not, you will find) Tj T*
(that questions and familiar discourse are best. For, 1. It keepeth) Tj T*
(them awake and attentive, when they know they must make some answer to) Tj T*
(your questions; which set speeches, with the dull and sluggish, will) Tj T*
(hardly do. 2. And it mightily helpeth them in the application; so that) Tj T*
(they much more easily take it home, and perceive themselves concerned) Tj T*
(in it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ VIII. Yet prudently take heed that you speak nothing to) Tj T*
(any in the presence of others, that tends to open their ignorance or) Tj T*
(sin, or the secrets of their hearts, or that any way tendeth to shame) Tj T*
(them \(except in the necessary reproof of the obstinate\). If it be) Tj T*
(their common ignorance that will be opened by questioning them, you) Tj T*
(may do it before your servants or children themselves, that are) Tj T*
(familiar with each other, but not when any strangers are present. But) Tj T*
(if it be about the secret state of their souls that you examine them,) Tj T*
(you must do it singly, when the person is alone. Lest shaming and) Tj T*
(troubling them make them hate instruction, and deprive them of all the) Tj T*
(benefit of it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ IX. When you come to teach them the doctrine of) Tj T*
(religion, begin with the baptismal covenant, as the sum of all that is) Tj T*
