() Tj T*
(_Answ._ 1. It is principally the rule to guide our inward) Tj T*
(desires, and outward expressions of them; both for the matter, what we) Tj T*
(must desire, and for the order which we must desire first and most. 2.) Tj T*
(But this rule is given in a form of words, most apt to express the) Tj T*
(said matter and order. 3. And this form may fitly be used in due) Tj T*
(season by all, and more necessarily by some. 4. But it was never) Tj T*
(intended to be the only words which we must use, no more than the) Tj T*
(creed is the only words that we must use to express the doctrine of) Tj T*
(faith, or the decalogue the only words to express our duty by.[57]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Quest._ II. What need is there of any other words of prayer, if) Tj T*
(the Lord's prayer be perfect?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ Because it is only a perfect summary, containing but the) Tj T*
(general heads: and it is needful to be more particular in our desires;) Tj T*
(for universals exist in particulars; and he that only nameth the) Tj T*
(general, and then another and another general, doth remember but few) Tj T*
(of the particulars. He that shall say, "I have sinned, and broken all) Tj T*
(thy commandments," doth generally confess every sin; but it is not) Tj T*
(true repentance, if it be not particular, for this, and that, and the) Tj T*
(other sin; at least as to the greater which may be remembered. He that) Tj T*
(shall say, "I believe all the word of God, or I believe in God the) Tj T*
(Father, Son, and Holy Ghost," may know little what is in the word of) Tj T*
(God, or what these generals signify, and therefore our faith must be) Tj T*
(more particular. So must desires after grace be particular also:) Tj T*
(otherwise it were enough to ask for mercy in the general. If you say,) Tj T*
(that God knoweth what those general words signify, though we do not; I) Tj T*
(answer, this is the papists' silly argument for Latin prayers, God) Tj T*
(knoweth our desires without any expressions or prayers at all, and he) Tj T*
(knoweth our wants without our desires. But it followeth not that) Tj T*
(prayers or desires are unnecessary. The exercise of our own repentance) Tj T*
(and desire doth make us persons fit to receive forgiveness, and the) Tj T*
(grace desired; when the impenitent, and those that desire it not, are) Tj T*
(unfit. And it is no true repentance, when you say, "I am sorry that I) Tj T*
(have sinned," but you know not, or remember not, wherein you have) Tj T*
(sinned, nor what your sin is; and so repent not indeed of any one sin) Tj T*
(at all. And so it is no true desire, that reacheth not to the) Tj T*
(particular, necessary graces, which we must desire; though I know some) Tj T*
(few very quick, comprehensive minds can in a moment think of many) Tj T*
(particulars, when they use but general words; and I know that some) Tj T*
(smaller, less necessary things, may be generally passed over; and) Tj T*
(greater matters in a time of haste, or when we, besides those) Tj T*
(generals, do also use particular requests.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Quest._ III. Is it lawful to pray in a set form of words?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ Nothing but very great ignorance can make you really) Tj T*
(doubt of it.[58] Hath God any where forbid it? You will say, that it) Tj T*
(is enough that he hath not commanded it. I answer, that in general he) Tj T*
(hath commanded it to all whose edification it tendeth to, when he) Tj T*
(commandeth you, that all be done to edification; but he hath given no) Tj T*
