(_Quest._ IV. But are those forms lawful which are prescribed by) Tj T*
(others, and not by God?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ Yea; or else it would be unlawful for a child or scholar) Tj T*
(to use a form prescribed by his parents or master. And to think that a) Tj T*
(thing lawful doth presently become unlawful, because a parent, master,) Tj T*
(pastor, or prince doth prescribe it or command it, is a conceit that I) Tj T*
(will not wrong my reader so far as to suppose him guilty of. Indeed if) Tj T*
(an usurper, that hath no authority over us in such matters, do) Tj T*
(prescribe it, we are not bound to formal obedience, that is, to do it) Tj T*
(therefore because he commandeth it; but yet I may be bound to it on) Tj T*
(some other accounts; and though his command do not bind me, yet it) Tj T*
(maketh not the thing itself unlawful.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
