(and feeling of his sins and wants, and hath a tongue that is used to) Tj T*
(express his mind even in these matters, with readiness and facility,) Tj T*
(it will greatly hinder the fervour of such a man's affections, to tie) Tj T*
(himself to premeditated words: to say the contrary, is to speak) Tj T*
(against the common sense and experience of such speakers and their) Tj T*
(hearers. And let them that yet deride this as uncertain and) Tj T*
(inconsiderate praying, but mark themselves, whether they cannot if) Tj T*
(they be hungry beg for bread, or ask help of their physician, or) Tj T*
(lawyer, or landlord, or any other, as well without a learned or) Tj T*
(studied form as with it? Who knoweth not that it is true which the new) Tj T*
(philosopher saith: Cartes. de Passion. part i. art. 44. _Et cum) Tj T*
(inter loquendum solum cogitamus de sensu illius rei, quam dicere) Tj T*
(volumus, id facit ut moveamus linguam et labra celerius et melius,) Tj T*
(quam si cogitaremus ea movere omnibus modis requisitis ad proferenda) Tj T*
(eadem verba; quia habitus quem acquisivimus cum disceremus loqui,) Tj T*
(&c._ Turning the thoughts too solicitously from the matter to the) Tj T*
(words, doth not only mortify the prayers of many, and turn them into a) Tj T*
(dead form, but also maketh them more dry and barren even as to the) Tj T*
(words themselves. The heavy charge, and bitter, scornful words which) Tj T*
(have been too common in this age, against praying without a set form) Tj T*
(by some, and against praying with a book or form by others, is so) Tj T*
(dishonourable a symptom or diagnostic of the church's sickness, as) Tj T*
(must needs be matter of shame and sorrow to the sounder, understanding) Tj T*
(part. For it cannot be denied, but it proves men's understandings and) Tj T*
(charity to be both exceedingly low.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
