(_Quest._ VIII. Must we pray always when the Spirit moveth us, and) Tj T*
(only then, or as reason guideth us?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ There are two sorts of the Spirit's motions; the one is) Tj T*
(by extraordinary inspiration or impulse, as he moved the prophets and) Tj T*
(apostles, to reveal new laws, or precepts, or events, or to do some) Tj T*
(actions without respect to any other command than the inspiration) Tj T*
(itself. This christians are not now to expect, because experience) Tj T*
(telleth us that it is ceased; or if any should pretend to it as not) Tj T*
(yet ceased, in the prediction of events, and direction in some things) Tj T*
(otherwise indifferent, yet it is most certain that it is ceased as to) Tj T*
(legislation; for the Spirit itself hath already given us those laws,) Tj T*
(which he hath declared to be perfect, and unchangeable till the end of) Tj T*
(the world: the other sort of the Spirit's working, is not to make new) Tj T*
(laws or duties, but to guide and quicken us in the doing of that which) Tj T*
(is our duty before by the laws already made. And these are the motions) Tj T*
(that all true christians must now expect. By which you may see, that) Tj T*
(the Spirit and reason are not to be here disjoined, much less opposed.) Tj T*
(As reason sufficeth not without the Spirit, being dark and asleep; so) Tj T*
(the Spirit worketh not on the will but by the reason: he moveth not a) Tj T*
(man as a beast or stone, to do a thing he knoweth not why; but by) Tj T*
(illumination giveth him the soundest reason for the doing of it: and) Tj T*
(duty is first duty before we do it; and when by our own sin we forfeit) Tj T*
(the special motions or help of the Spirit, duty doth not thereby cease) Tj T*
(to be duty, nor our omission to be sin. If the Spirit of God teach you) Tj T*
(to discern the meetest season for prayer, by considering your affairs,) Tj T*
(and when you are most free, this is not to be denied to be the work of) Tj T*
(the Spirit, because it is rational \(as fanatic enthusiasts imagine\).) Tj T*
(And if you are moved to pray in a crowd of business, or at any time) Tj T*
(when reason can prove that it is not your duty but your sin, the same) Tj T*
(reason proveth that it was not the Spirit of God that moved you to it:) Tj T*
(for the Spirit in the heart is not contrary to the Spirit in the) Tj T*
(Scripture. Set upon the duty which the Spirit in the Scripture) Tj T*
(commandeth you, and then you may be sure that you obey the Spirit;) Tj T*
(otherwise you disobey it. Yea, if your hearts be cold, prayer is a) Tj T*
