(for lawful things to unlawful ends; and this is still abominable to) Tj T*
(God.[61] 2. A wicked man may have in him some good that proceedeth) Tj T*
(from common grace; and this he may be obliged to exercise, and so by) Tj T*
(prayer to express his desires so far as they are good. 3. A wicked) Tj T*
(man's wicked prayers are never accepted, but a wicked man's prayers) Tj T*
(which are for good things, from common grace, are so far accepted as) Tj T*
(that they are some means conducing to his reformation; and though his) Tj T*
(person be still unjustified, and these prayers sinful, yet the total) Tj T*
(omission of them is a greater sin. 4. A wicked man is bound at once to) Tj T*
(repent and pray, Acts viii. 22; Isa. lv. 6, 7. And whenever God bids) Tj T*
(him ask for grace, he bids him desire grace; and to bid him pray, is) Tj T*
(to bid him repent and be of a better mind: therefore those that) Tj T*
(reprove ministers for persuading wicked men to pray, reprove them for) Tj T*
(persuading them to repentance and good desires. But if they pray) Tj T*
