() Tj T*
(_Quest._ XIX. May we pray absolutely for outward mercies, or only) Tj T*
(conditionally?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ You must distinguish, 1. Between a condition spoken of) Tj T*
(the subject, when we are uncertain whether it be a mercy or not, and) Tj T*
(an extrinsic condition of the grant. 2. Between a condition of prayer,) Tj T*
(and a condition of expectation. 3. Between submission to God's will,) Tj T*
(and a conditional desire or prayer. And so I answer, \(1.\) It is) Tj T*
(necessary when we are uncertain whether the thing itself be good or) Tj T*
(not, that we pray with a subjective conditionality: Grant this if it) Tj T*
(be good; or, If it be not good I do not pray for it. For it is) Tj T*
(presupposed in prayer that we know the thing prayed for to be good.) Tj T*
(\(2.\) But when we know the thing to be a mercy and good, we may pray) Tj T*
(for it absolutely. \(3.\) But we may not believe that we shall receive) Tj T*
(all with an absolute expectation, which we absolutely pray for. For) Tj T*
(prayer being the expression of desire, that which may be absolutely) Tj T*
(desired, though not absolutely promised, may be absolutely prayed for.) Tj T*
(\(As our increase or strength of grace, or the conversion of our) Tj T*
(relations, &c.\) \(4.\) But yet all such must be asked with a submission) Tj T*
(to the will of God: but that maketh it not properly a conditional form) Tj T*
(of praying; for when the nature of prayer is as it were to move the) Tj T*
(will of God, it is not so proper to say, Lord, do this if it be thy) Tj T*
(will already; or, Lord, be pleased to do this if it be thy pleasure;) Tj T*
(as to say, Lord, grant this mercy; but if thou deny it, it is my duty) Tj T*
(to submit. So Christ mentioned both the subjective conditionality and) Tj T*
(the submission of his will. Matt. xxvi. 39, "If it be possible, let) Tj T*
(this cup pass from me: nevertheless not as I will, but as thou wilt.") Tj T*
(As if he had said, Nature requireth me with a simple nolition to be) Tj T*
(unwilling of the suffering, and if it be consistent with the desired) Tj T*
(ends of my mediatorship, to be desirous to avoid it; but seeing that) Tj T*
(cannot be, my comparing will commandeth this simple will of) Tj T*
(self-preservation to submit to thy most perfect will. But if any call) Tj T*
(this \(submission\) a condition, the matter is not great.) Tj T*
