(_Answ._ No: for prayer is not only an expression of desire, but) Tj T*
(also a means to attain the thing desired. And some things may be) Tj T*
(lawfully desired, \(at least with a simple velleity,\) which may not be) Tj T*
(sought, because they must not be hoped for, where God hath said that) Tj T*
(he will not grant them. For it is vain to seek that which you have no) Tj T*
(hope to find: as to desire to see the conversion of the whole world,) Tj T*
(or to pass to heaven as Enoch without dying, are lawful \(by a simple) Tj T*
(velleity\); but all things compared, it is not lawful peremptorily to) Tj T*
(desire it, without submission; and therefore not to ask it. It is the) Tj T*
(expression of a comparate, determinate desire, which is properly) Tj T*
(called prayer, being the use of means for the obtaining of that) Tj T*
(desire; and whatsoever I may so desire, I may pray for; for if there) Tj T*
(be no hope of it, I may not so desire it. But the desire by way of) Tj T*
(simple velleity may not be put into a proper prayer, when there is no) Tj T*
(hope. I must have a simple desire \(with submission\) to attain a) Tj T*
(sinless perfection here, even this hour; but because there is no hope,) Tj T*
(I may not let it proceed to a determinate peremptory desire upon a) Tj T*
(comparing judgment, nor into a proper prayer. And yet these velleities) Tj T*
(may be expressed in prayer, though they have not the full nature of a) Tj T*
(prayer. _Object._ But was not Christ's a prayer? Matt. xxvi. 39.) Tj T*
(_Answ._ Either Christ as man was certain that the cup must not) Tj T*
(pass from him, or uncertain. If you could prove him uncertain, then it) Tj T*
(is a proper prayer \(with submission to his Father's will\); but if he) Tj T*
(was certain that it was not to pass from him, then it was analogically) Tj T*
(only a prayer, it being but a representing of his velleity to his) Tj T*
(Father, and not of his determinate will, nor was any means to attain) Tj T*
(that end: and indeed such it was, as if he had said, Father, if it had) Tj T*
(stood with the ends of my office and thy will, I would have asked this) Tj T*
(of thee; but because it doth not, I submit. And this much we may do.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Quest._ XXI. How then can we pray for the salvation of all the) Tj T*
(world? must it be for all men collectively? or only for some, excluding) Tj T*
(no numerical denominate person?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ Just as Christ prayed here in this text, we must express) Tj T*
(our simple velleity of it to God, as a thing that in itself is most) Tj T*
(desirable \(as the passing of the cup was unto Christ\): but we cannot) Tj T*
(express a determinate volition, by a full prayer, such as has any) Tj T*
(tendency as a means to attain that end; because we are certain that) Tj T*
