(_Direct._ III. Take heed of doctrines of presumption and) Tj T*
(security, and take heed lest you fall away, by thinking it so) Tj T*
(impossible to fall away, that you are past all danger.[94] The) Tj T*
(covenant of grace doth sufficiently encourage you to obey and hope,) Tj T*
(against temptations to despair and casting off the means: but it) Tj T*
(encourageth no man to presume or sin, or to cast off means as needless) Tj T*
(things. Remember that if ever you will stand, the fear of falling must) Tj T*
(help you to stand; and if ever you will persevere, it must be by) Tj T*
(seeing the danger of backsliding, so far as to make you afraid, and) Tj T*
(quicken you in the means which are necessary to prevent it. It is no) Tj T*
(more certain that you shall persevere, than it is certain that you) Tj T*
(shall use the means of persevering: and one means is, by seeing your) Tj T*
(danger, to be stirred up to fear and caution to escape it. Because it) Tj T*
(is my meaning in this direction, to save men from perishing by) Tj T*
(security upon the abuse of the doctrine of perseverance, I hope none) Tj T*
(will be offended that I lay down these antidotes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. Consider, that the doctrine of perseverance hath nothing in it to) Tj T*
(encourage security. The very controversies about it, may cause you to) Tj T*
(conclude, that a certain sin is not to be built upon a controverted) Tj T*
(doctrine. Till Augustine's time, it is hard to find any ancient) Tj T*
(writers, that clearly asserted the certain perseverance of any at all.) Tj T*
(Augustine and Prosper maintain the certain perseverance of all the) Tj T*
(elect, but deny the certain perseverance of all that are regenerated,) Tj T*
(justified, or sanctified; for they thought that more were regenerate) Tj T*
(and justified than were elect, of whom some stood \(even all the elect\)) Tj T*
(and the rest fell away: so that I confess, I never read one ancient) Tj T*
(father, or christian writer, that ever maintained the certainty of) Tj T*
(the perseverance of all the justified, of many hundred, if not a) Tj T*
(thousand years after Christ. And a doctrine, that to the church was so) Tj T*
(long unknown, hath not that certainty, or that necessity, as to) Tj T*
(encourage you to any presumption or security. The churches were saved) Tj T*
(many hundred years without believing it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. The doctrine of perseverance is against security, because it) Tj T*
(uniteth together the end and the means: for they that teach that the) Tj T*
(justified shall never totally fall from grace, do also teach that they) Tj T*
(shall never totally fall into security, or to any reigning sin; for) Tj T*
(this is to fall away from grace. And they teach that they shall never) Tj T*
(totally fall from the use of the necessary means of their) Tj T*
(preservation; nor from the cautelous avoiding of the danger of their) Tj T*
(souls: God doth not simply decree that you shall persevere; but that) Tj T*
(you shall be kept in perseverance by the fear of your danger, and the) Tj T*
(careful use of means; and that you shall persevere in these, as well) Tj T*
(as in other graces. Therefore if you fall to security and sin, you) Tj T*
(fall away from grace, and show that God never decreed or promised that) Tj T*
(you should never fall away.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. Consider how far many have gone that have fallen away: the) Tj T*
(instances of our times are much higher than any I can name to you out) Tj T*
(of history. Men that have seemed to walk humbly and holily, fearing) Tj T*
(all sin, blameless in their lives, zealous in religion, twenty or) Tj T*
