(I HAVE said so much of this already, part i. about covetousness) Tj T*
(or worldliness, and about good works, and in my book of "Self-denial,") Tj T*
(and that of "Crucifying the World;" that my reason commandeth me) Tj T*
(brevity in this place.[114]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ I. Remember that riches are no part of your felicity;) Tj T*
(or that if you have no better, you are undone men. Dare you say that) Tj T*
(they are fit to make you happy? Dare you say, that you will take them) Tj T*
(for your part? and be content to be turned off when they forsake you?) Tj T*
(They reconcile not God; they save not from his wrath; they heal not a) Tj T*
(wounded conscience: they may please your flesh, and adorn your) Tj T*
(funeral, but they neither delay, nor sanctify, nor sweeten death, nor) Tj T*
(make you either better or happier than the poor. Riches are nothing) Tj T*
(but plentiful provision for tempting, corruptible flesh. When the) Tj T*
(flesh is in the dust, it is rich no more. All that abounded in wealth,) Tj T*
(since Adam's days till now, are levelled with the lowest in the dust.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ II. Yea, remember that riches are not the smallest) Tj T*
(temptation and danger to your souls. Do they delight and please you?) Tj T*
