(By that way they may destroy you. If they be but loved above God, and) Tj T*
(make earth seem better for you than heaven, they have undone you. And) Tj T*
(if God recover you not, it had been better for you to have been worms) Tj T*
(or brutes, than such deceived, miserable souls. It is not for nothing,) Tj T*
(that Christ giveth you so many terrible warnings about riches, and so) Tj T*
(describeth the folly, the danger, and the misery of the worldly rich,) Tj T*
(Luke xii. 17-20; xvi. 19-21, &c; xviii. 21-23, &c.; and telleth you) Tj T*
(how hardly the rich are saved. Fire burneth most, when it hath most) Tj T*
(fuel; and riches are the fuel of worldly love and fleshly lust, 1 John) Tj T*
(ii. 15, 16; Rom. xiii. 13, 14.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ III. Understand what it is to love and trust in worldly) Tj T*
(prosperity and wealth. Many here deceive themselves to their) Tj T*
(destruction. They persuade themselves, that they desire and use their) Tj T*
(riches but for necessity: but that they do not love them, nor trust in) Tj T*
(them, because they can say that heaven is better, and wealth will) Tj T*
(leave us to a grave! But do you not love that ease, that greatness,) Tj T*
(that domination, that fulness, that satisfaction of your appetite,) Tj T*
(eye, and fancy, which you cannot have without your wealth? It is) Tj T*
(fleshly lust, and will, and pleasure, which carnal worldlings love for) Tj T*
(itself; and then they love their wealth for these. And to trust in) Tj T*
(riches, is not to trust that they will never leave you; for every fool) Tj T*
(doth know the contrary. But it is to rest, and quiet, and comfort your) Tj T*
(minds in them, as that which most pleaseth you, and maketh you well,) Tj T*
(or to be as you would be. Like him in Luke xii. 18, 19, that said,) Tj T*
("Soul, take thy ease, eat, drink, and be merry, thou hast enough laid) Tj T*
(up for many years." This is to love and trust in riches.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ IV. Above all the deceits and dangers of this world,) Tj T*
(take heed of a secret, hypocritical hope of reconciling the world to) Tj T*
(heaven, so as to make you a felicity of both; and dreaming of a) Tj T*
(compounded portion, or of serving God and mammon.[115] The true state) Tj T*
(of the hypocrite's heart and hope is, to love his worldly prosperity) Tj T*
(best, and desire to keep it as long as he can, for the enjoyment of) Tj T*
(his fleshly pleasures; and when he must leave this world against his) Tj T*
(will, he hopeth then to have heaven as his reserve; because he) Tj T*
(thinketh it better than hell, and his tongue can say, It is better) Tj T*
(than earth, though his will and affections say the contrary. If this) Tj T*
(be your case, the Lord have mercy upon you, and give you a more) Tj T*
(believing, spiritual mind, or else you are lost, and you and your) Tj T*
(treasure will perish together.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ V. Accordingly take heed, lest when you seem to resign) Tj T*
(yourselves, and all that you have, to God, there should be a secret) Tj T*
(purpose at the heart, that you will never be undone in the world for) Tj T*
(Christ, nor for the hopes of a better world. A knowing hypocrite is) Tj T*
(not ignorant, that the terms of Christ, proposed in the gospel, Luke) Tj T*
(xiv. 26, 27, 33, are no lower than forsaking all; and that in baptism,) Tj T*
(and our covenant with Christ, all must be designed and devoted to him,) Tj T*
(and the cross taken up instead of all, or else we are no christians,) Tj T*
(as being not in covenant with Christ. But the hypocrite's hope is,) Tj T*
(that though Christ put him upon these promises, he will never put him) Tj T*
