() Tj T*
(_Direct._ VI. And accordingly take heed lest while you pretend to) Tj T*
(live to God, and to use all that you have as his stewards for his) Tj T*
(service, you should deceitfully put him off with the leavings of your) Tj T*
(lusts, and give him only so much as your flesh can spare. It is not) Tj T*
(likely that the damned gentleman, Luke xvi. was never used to give any) Tj T*
(thing to the poor; else what did beggars use his doors for? When) Tj T*
(Christ promiseth to reward men for a cup of cold water, the meaning) Tj T*
(is, when they would give better if they had it. There are few rich men) Tj T*
(of all that go to hell, that were so void of human compassion, or of) Tj T*
(the sense of their own reputation, as to give nothing at all to the) Tj T*
(poor; but God will have all, though not all for the poor, yet all) Tj T*
(employed as he commandeth; and will not be put off with your tithes or) Tj T*
(scraps. His stewards confess that they have nothing of their own.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ VII. Let the use of your riches in prosperity show, that you) Tj T*
(do not dissemble when you promise to forsake all for Christ in trial,) Tj T*
(rather than forsake him. You may know whether you are true or false in) Tj T*
(your covenant with Christ, and what you would do in a day of trial, by) Tj T*
(what you do in your daily course of life. How can that man leave all) Tj T*
(at once for Christ, that cannot daily serve him with his riches, nor) Tj T*
(leave that little which God requireth, in the discharge of his duty in) Tj T*
(pious and charitable works? What is it to leave all for God, but to) Tj T*
(leave all rather than to sin against God? And will he do that, who) Tj T*
(daily sinneth against God by omission of good works, because he) Tj T*
(cannot leave some part? Study, as faithful stewards, to serve God to) Tj T*
(the utmost with what you have now, and then you may expect that his) Tj T*
(grace should enable you to leave all in trial, and not prove withering) Tj T*
(hypocrites and apostates.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ VIII. Be not rich to yourselves, or to your fleshly) Tj T*
(wills and lusts;[116] but remember that the rich are bound to be) Tj T*
(spiritual, and to mortify the flesh, as well as the poor. Let lust) Tj T*
(fare never the better for all the fulness of your estates. Fast and) Tj T*
(humble your souls never the less; please an inordinate appetite never) Tj T*
(the more in meat and drink; live never the more in unprofitable) Tj T*
(idleness. The rich must labour as constantly as the poor, though not) Tj T*
(in the same kind of work. The rich must live soberly, temperately, and) Tj T*
(heavenly, and must as much mortify all fleshly desires, as the poor.) Tj T*
(You have the same law and Master, and have no more liberty to indulge) Tj T*
(your lusts; but if you live after the flesh, you shall die as well as) Tj T*
(any other. Oh the partiality of carnal minds! They can see the fault) Tj T*
(of a poor man, that goeth sometimes to an ale-house, who perhaps) Tj T*
(drinketh water \(or that which is next to it\) all the week; when they) Tj T*
(never blame themselves, who scarce miss a meal without wine and strong) Tj T*
