(drink, and eating that which their appetite desireth. They think it a) Tj T*
(crime in a poor man, to spend but one day in many in such idleness, as) Tj T*
(they themselves spend most of their lives in. Gentlemen think that) Tj T*
(their riches allow them to live without any profitable labour, and to) Tj T*
(gratify their flesh, and fare deliciously every day; as if it were) Tj T*
(their privilege to be sensual, and to be damned, Rom. viii. 1, 5-9, 13.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ IX. Nay, remember that you are called to far greater) Tj T*
(self-denial, and fear, and watchfulness against sensuality, and) Tj T*
(wealthy vices, than the poor are. Mortification is as necessary to) Tj T*
(your salvation, as to theirs, but much more difficult. If you live) Tj T*
(after the flesh, you shall die as well as they. And how much stronger) Tj T*
(are your temptations! Is not he easilier drawn to gluttony or excess) Tj T*
(in quality or quantity, who hath daily a table of plenty, and) Tj T*
(enticing, delicious food before him, than he that never seeth such a) Tj T*
(temptation once in half a year? Is it not harder for him to deny his) Tj T*
(appetite who hath the baits of pleasant meats and drinks daily set) Tj T*
(upon his table, than for him that is seldom in sight of them, and) Tj T*
(perhaps in no possibility of procuring them; and therefore hath) Tj T*
(nothing to solicit his appetite or thoughts? Doubtless the rich, if) Tj T*
(ever they will be saved, must watch more constantly, and set a more) Tj T*
(resolute guard upon the flesh, and live more in fear of sensuality,) Tj T*
(than the poor, as they live in greater temptations and dangers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ X. Know therefore particularly what are the temptations) Tj T*
(of prosperity, that you may make a particular, prosperous resistance.) Tj T*
(And they are especially these:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. Pride. The foolish heart of man is apt to swell upon the accession) Tj T*
(of so poor a matter as wealth; and men think they are got above their) Tj T*
(neighbours, and more honour and obeisance is their due, if they be but) Tj T*
(richer.[117]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. Fulness of bread.[118] If they do not eat till they are sick, they) Tj T*
(think the constant and costly pleasing of their appetite in meats and) Tj T*
(drinks, is lawful.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. Idleness. They think he is not bound to labour, that can live) Tj T*
(without it, and hath enough.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. Time-wasting sports and recreations. They think their hours may be) Tj T*
(devoted to the flesh, when all their lives are devoted to it; they) Tj T*
(think their wealth alloweth them to play, and court, and compliment) Tj T*
(away that precious time, which no men have more need to redeem; they) Tj T*
(tell God that he hath given them more time than they have need of; and) Tj T*
(God will shortly cut it off, and tell them that they shall have no) Tj T*
(more.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5. Lust and wantonness, fulness and idleness, cherish both the) Tj T*
(cogitations and inclinations unto filthiness; they that live in) Tj T*
(gluttony and drunkenness, are like to live in chambering and) Tj T*
(wantonness.[119]) Tj T*
