() Tj T*
(6. Curiosity, and wasting their lives in a multitude of little,) Tj T*
(ceremonious, unprofitable things, to the exclusion of the great) Tj T*
(businesses of life.[120] Well may we say, that men's lusts are their) Tj T*
(jailors, and their fetters, when we see to what a wretched kind of) Tj T*
(life a multitude of the rich \(especially ladies and gentlewomen\) do) Tj T*
(condemn themselves. I should pity one in bridewell, that were but tied) Tj T*
(so to spend their time; when they have poor, ignorant, proud, worldly,) Tj T*
(peevish, hypocritical, ungodly souls to be healed, and a life of great) Tj T*
(and weighty business to do for eternity, they have so many little) Tj T*
(things all day to do, that leave them little time to converse with) Tj T*
(God, or with their consciences, or to do any thing that is really) Tj T*
(worth the living for: they have so many fine clothes and ornaments to) Tj T*
(get, and use; and so many rooms to beautify and adorn, and so many) Tj T*
(servants to talk with, that attend them, and so many dishes and sauces) Tj T*
(to bespeak, and so many flowers to plant, and dress, and walks, and) Tj T*
(places of pleasure to mind; and so many visitors to entertain with) Tj T*
(whole hours of unprofitable talk; and so many great persons) Tj T*
(accordingly to visit; and so many laws of ceremony and compliment to) Tj T*
(observe; and so many games to play, \(perhaps,\) and so many hours to) Tj T*
(sleep, that the day, the year, their lives are gone, before they could) Tj T*
(have while to know what they lived for. And if God had but damned them) Tj T*
(to spend their days in picking straws or filling a bottomless vessel,) Tj T*
(or to spend their days as they choose themselves to spend them, it) Tj T*
(would have tempted us to think him unmerciful to his creatures.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(7. Tyranny and oppression: when men are above others, how commonly do) Tj T*
(they think that their wills must be fulfilled by all men, and none) Tj T*
(must cross them, and they live as if all others below them were as) Tj T*
(their beasts, that are made for them, to serve and please them.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ XI. Let your fruitfulness to God, and the public good,) Tj T*
(be proportionable to your possessions.[121] Do as much more good in) Tj T*
(the world than the poor, as you are better furnished with it than) Tj T*
(they. Let your servants have more time for the learning of God's word,) Tj T*
(and let your families be the more religiously instructed and governed.) Tj T*
(To whom God giveth much, from them he doth expect much.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Direct._ XII. Do not only take occasions of doing good, when they are) Tj T*
(thrust upon you; but study how to do all the good you can, as those) Tj T*
("that are zealous of good works," Tit. ii. 14.[122] Zeal of good works) Tj T*
(will make you, 1. Plot and contrive for them. 2. Consult and ask) Tj T*
(advice for them. 3. It will make you glad when you meet with a hopeful) Tj T*
(opportunity. 4. It will make you do it largely, and not sparingly, and) Tj T*
(by the halves. 5. It will make you do it speedily, without unwilling) Tj T*
(backwardness and delay. 6. It will make you do it constantly to your) Tj T*
(lives' end. 7. It will make you pinch your own flesh, and suffer) Tj T*
(somewhat yourselves to do good to others. 8. It will make you labour) Tj T*
(in it as your trade, and not only consent that others do good at your) Tj T*
(charge. 9. It will make you glad when good is done, and not to grudge) Tj T*
(at what it cost you. 10. In a word, it will make your neighbours to be) Tj T*
