(_Quest._ I. But is it a duty when the sick are like to die, to) Tj T*
(make it known to them?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ Sometimes it is, and sometimes not. 1. Some sicknesses) Tj T*
(are such, as will be so increased with fear, that the patient that) Tj T*
(before was in hope of a recovery, will be put almost past hope. And) Tj T*
(some sicknesses are much different, and are not like to be so) Tj T*
(increased by it. And some are past all hope already. 2. Some are so) Tj T*
(prepared to die, that they have the less need to be acquainted with) Tj T*
(their danger; and some are unconverted, and in so dangerous a case,) Tj T*
(that the absolute necessity of their souls may require it. When the) Tj T*
(soul is in so sad a case, and yet the body may be endangered by the) Tj T*
(fear of the sentence of death, it is the safest course to tell them,) Tj T*
(that though God may recover them, yet their disease is so dangerous,) Tj T*
(as calleth for their speedy and serious preparation for death; which) Tj T*
(will not be lost, if God restore them. So that they may have so much) Tj T*
(hope, as to keep their fear from killing them, and so much) Tj T*
(acquaintance with their danger, as may put them upon their duty. But) Tj T*
(in case there be already little or no hope, or in case the disease) Tj T*
(will be but little increased by the fear, \(which is the case of the) Tj T*
(most,\) the danger should not at all be hid.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Quest._ II. Am I always bound to tell a wicked man of his sin) Tj T*
(and misery, when it may exasperate his disease, and offend his mind?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ If it were a sickness that is void of danger, in case his mind) Tj T*
(be quiet, and be like to kill him if his mind be disturbed, then it) Tj T*
(were the most prudent course to call him so far to repentance and) Tj T*
(faith, as you can do it without any dangerous disturbance of him;) Tj T*
(because it is most charity to his soul to help him to a longer time of) Tj T*
(repentance, rather than to lay all the hopes of his salvation upon the) Tj T*
(present time. But this is not an ordinary case; therefore ordinarily) Tj T*
(it is a duty to acquaint the sick person, that is yet in his sin, and) Tj T*
(unregenerate state, with the truth of his danger, and the necessity) Tj T*
(of renovation. Alas! it is a lamentable kind of friendship, to flatter) Tj T*
(a poor soul into damnation, or to hide his danger till he is past) Tj T*
(recovery. When he is in a state of unexpressible misery, and hath but) Tj T*
(a few days' or weeks' time left, to do all that ever must be done for) Tj T*
(his salvation; what horrid cruelty is it then, to let him go to hell) Tj T*
(for fear of displeasing or disquieting him!) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Object._ But I am afraid I shall cast him into despair, if I) Tj T*
(tell him plainly that he is in a state of damnation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Answ._ If you let him alone a little longer, he will be in) Tj T*
(remediless despair. There is no despair remediless, but that in hell.) Tj T*
(But now you may help to save him, both from present and endless) Tj T*
(desperation. He must needs despair of ever being saved without a) Tj T*
(Christ, or without the regeneration of the Holy Spirit, or without) Tj T*
(true faith and repentance, and love to God, and holiness. But need he) Tj T*
(despair of attaining all these, while Christ is offered him so freely,) Tj T*
